{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":1203,"output":1043,"cacheRead":21891,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":24137,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0,"totalFromProvider":false}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Ha — the log is my own current session being recorded (run4). So no prior attempt content? Well, run4 means previous runs 1-3 existed but their session jsonl aren't there... Actually only one jsonl for this task exists, which is this current run (the sessions dir lists run4). Fine.\n\nSo the prior attempts (1-3) presumably failed without session files, or the task file was just moved to tasks/. Anyway, let's do the work.\n\nDesign decisions:\n1. `CategoriesSidebar` should require `onOpenRestore` (remove default no-op) and HomeScreen passes `onOpenRestore` callback that navigates to the Restore route. But note: in HomeScreen, sidebarOpen is a local rememberSaveable state; navigating to Restore route would replace HomeScreen. That's fine — same behaviour as TransactionHistory from Home. But we should also close the sidebar. Let's just call onClose and navigate.\n\n2. HomeScreen's two balance-row IconButtons: change onClick to onOpenTransactionHistory and icon to `Icons.Outlined.Menu` (3 horizontal lines hamburger), contentDescription \"Open transaction history\".\n\n3. Also add testTags for these buttons to allow UI test selection: e.g. `balance_side_left` / `balance_side_right`? Existing pattern uses testTag for buttons (add_expense_button). Adding tags is reasonable and helps the androidTest.\n\nNow tests: verification runs `./gradlew testDebugUnitTest` (JVM). But test_globs include androidTest. The require_tests check just requires a test file change. UI behaviour can be tested in androidTest (instrumentation) — but harness only runs unit tests. Still, per AGENTS.md, UI tests live in androidTest and the smoke test exists there. A good approach: write both:\n- A JVM unit test that verifies wiring at a level testable in JVM: e.g. a test asserting that CategoriesSidebar's `onOpenRestore` parameter has no default no-op and HomeScreen passes it — that's source-inspection style. Hmm, precedent exists (GradleDeprecationWarningsTest reads source files). But is that appropriate? An alternative, more meaningful JVM test: extract the sidebar menu items definition into a testable pure function/data structure, e.g. `SidebarMenu` with entries list including Restore action, then test that clicking the Restore entry invokes the callback. That's over-engineering though.\n\nBetter plan: make the wiring testable genuinely by introducing a small seam in HomeScreen? Compose composables can't run in plain JVM tests without Robolectric (not present).\n\nConsider adding Robolectric + compose ui test to unit tests? That'd be a big build change, maybe risky (downloads dependencies; offline?). Might break the harness verify. Not ideal.\n\nPrecedent in this repo: `TransactionHistoryScreenTest.kt` in app/src/test tests pure grouping logic — a \"screen test\" that's really logic tests. And androidTest has BudgetAppSmokeTest that uses compose UI test against MainActivity. So the repo pattern for UI behaviour is androidTest instrumentation tests. I'll add an androidTest for the restore/sidebar behaviour, plus a JVM unit test that guards the wiring at the source level? Hmm.\n\nActually, there's a cleaner JVM-testable design: put the Home screen's balance-side button action and sidebar restore wiring into the ViewModel/navigation layer? Not really.\n\nAlternative approach that makes the fix genuinely unit-testable: refactor navigation callbacks in HomeScreen behind a small `HomeNavigation` abstraction? Too much.\n\nHmm. Let me think about what's testable in JVM: the sidebar Restore click invokes the `onOpenRestore` callback passed to CategoriesSidebar. If I make `onOpenRestore` a required parameter (no default), the bug (missing wiring) becomes a compile error rather than a silent no-op — that's the actual root-cause fix. A JVM unit test could then... not test composability.\n\nBut I could restructure so the sidebar menu items are data: e.g.\n\n```kotlin\nsealed interface SidebarAction { ... }\n```\nOverkill.\n\nLet me check whether the repo has any compose-in-JVM tests already. Search test files for \"compose\" or \"robolectric\".\n"}}
