eTestRule.onNodeWithText(\"Restore\").performClick()\n        composeTestRule.onNodeWithText(\"Manual CSV restore\").assertIsDisplayed()\n    }\n\n    @Test fun balanceSideButtonsOpenTransactionHistory() {\n        composeTestRule.onNodeWithContentDescription(\"Open transaction history\", useUnmergedTree = true).assertCount() == 2 ... \n        composeTestRule.onNodeWithContentDescription(\"Open transaction history\").onFirst().performClick()\n        composeTestRule.onNodeWithText(\"Transactions\").assertIsDisplayed()\n    }\n\n    @Test fun homeScreenHasNoRestoreIconButtons() {\n        composeTestRule.onNodeWithContentDescription(\"Open restore\").assertDoesNotExist()\n    }\n}\n```\n\nNote: on the Home screen, the sidebar isn't open initially, so \"Restore\" text doesn't exist → good. Also `onNodeWithContentDescription(\"Open restore\")` — TransactionHistoryScreen uses that contentDescription for its MoreVert action; but on Home it should not exist.\n\nCareful: the sidebar slide-in animation may need waiting; Compose test's performClick on a node after AnimatedVisibility... The test framework waits for idle. Should be fine; maybe use `waitUntil`.\n\nAlso the androidTest will use MainActivity which needs the app's Room DB — BudgetAppSmokeTest already does this, so fine.\n\nNow the JVM guard test. Name: `app/src/test/java/com/lorenzo/monefyme/ui/screen/HomeScreenSidebarWiringTest.kt`? Hmm, maybe better placed in a `ui/wiring` package? Keep it in `com.lorenzo.monefyme.ui.screen`.\n\nActually — think harder about a genuinely behavioural JVM test. What about testing CategoriesSidebar menu structure? The MenuBody composable has two entries hardcoded. I could extract the menu entries as data:\n\n```kotlin\ninternal data class SidebarMenuItem(val label: String, val action: () -> Unit)\n```\nand build `sidebarMenuItems(onOpenCategories, onOpenRestore)` as a pure function that MenuBody renders from a list. Then a JVM unit test can call that function and assert the Restore