{"type":"turn_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"\"Transactions\" title identifies the TransactionHistoryScreen.\n\nNow implement the edits.\n\nHomeScreen edit 1: imports — remove `Icons.Outlined.Restore`, add `Icons.Outlined.Menu`.\n\nCurrent imports:\n```\nimport androidx.compose.material.icons.outlined.MoreVert\nimport androidx.compose.material.icons.outlined.Restore\n```\nReplace Restore with Menu — but alphabetical order: ChevronRight, Menu, MoreVert, Restore... Order currently: Close, ChevronLeft, ChevronRight, MoreVert, Restore. Insert Menu before MoreVert and drop Restore.\n\nEdit 2 & 3: the two IconButtons.\n\nThey're identical blocks; oldText must be unique. The left one is preceded by `horizontalArrangement = Arrangement.SpaceBetween,\\n        ) {\\n            IconButton(` and right one is preceded by the Surface block closing. Let me use surrounding context to disambiguate.\n\nLeft block:\n```\n            horizontalArrangement = Arrangement.SpaceBetween,\n        ) {\n            IconButton(\n                onClick = onOpenRestore,\n                modifier = Modifier.size(40.dp),\n            ) {\n                Icon(\n                    imageVector = Icons.Outlined.Restore,\n                    contentDescription = \"Open restore\",\n                    tint = Color(0xFF7CC8A4),\n                )\n            }\n            Surface(\n```\n\nRight block:\n```\n            IconButton(\n                onClick = onOpenRestore,\n                modifier = Modifier.size(40.dp),\n            ) {\n                Icon(\n                    imageVector = Icons.Outlined.Restore,\n                    contentDescription = \"Open restore\",\n                    tint = Color(0xFF7CC8A4),\n                )\n            }\n        }\n\n        Row(\n            modifier = Modifier\n                .fillMaxWidth()\n                .navigationBarsPadding()\n```\n\nGood, both unique with context.\n\nEdit 4: sidebar call — add onOpenRestore param:\n```\n            CategoriesSidebar(\n                categories = allCategories,\n                onAddCategory = viewModel::saveCategory,\n                onEditCategory = viewModel::updateCategory,\n                onDeleteCategory = viewModel::deleteCategory,\n                onClose = { sidebarOpen = false },\n            )\n```\n→ add `onOpenRestore = { sidebarOpen = false; onOpenRestore() },`\n\nHmm, but onOpenRestore is the HomeScreen param name; inside lambda referencing `onOpenRestore` works.\n\nCategoriesSidebar: change `onOpenRestore: () -> Unit = {},` → `onOpenRestore: () -> Unit,`\n\nAlso maybe give the sidebar Restore row a testTag for the UI test: `testTag(\"sidebar_restore\")`. Optional; the text node works. But for robustness in androidTest, use onNodeWithText(\"Restore\") — hmm, Restore screen's top bar title is also \"Restore\" so a click test asserting navigation could match either. Better assert \"Manual CSV restore\" text displayed. That's fine.\n\nAlso I should add testTags on the balance-side buttons for the androidTest to assert icon/click: `Modifier.testTag(\"balance_history_left\")`. Hmm, but asserting the icon type in Compose tests isn't straightforward. We can assert contentDescription: onNodeWithContentDescription(\"Open transaction history\") — count 2, and assert no node with contentDescription \"Open restore\" on Home. That verifies the icon swap effectively.\n\nLet's write the androidTest: `HomeScreenRestoreButtonsTest.kt`:\n\n```kotlin\nclass HomeScreenSidebarRestoreTest {\n    @get:Rule val composeTestRule = createAndroidComposeRule<MainActivity>()\n\n    @Test fun sidebarRestoreButtonOpensRestoreScreen() {\n        composeTestRule.onNodeWithContentDescription(\"More\").performClick()\n        composeTestRule.onNodeWithText(\"Restore\").performClick()\n        composeTestRule.onNodeWithText(\"Manual CSV restore\").assertIsDisplayed()\n    }\n\n    @Test fun balanceSideButtonsOpenTransactionHistory() {\n        composeTestRule.onNodeWithContentDescription(\"Open transaction history\", useUnmergedTree = true).assertCount() == 2 ... \n        composeTestRule.onNodeWithContentDescription(\"Open transaction history\").onFirst().performClick()\n        composeTestRule.onNodeWithText(\"Transactions\").assertIsDisplayed()\n    }\n\n    @Test fun homeScreenHasNoRestoreIconButtons() {\n        composeTestRule.onNodeWithContentDescription(\"Open restore\").assertDoesNotExist()\n    }\n}\n```\n\nNote: on the Home screen, the sidebar isn't open initially, so \"Restore\" text doesn't exist → good. Also `onNodeWithContentDescription(\"Open restore\")` — TransactionHistoryScreen uses that contentDescription for its MoreVert action; but on Home it should not exist.\n\nCareful: the sidebar slide-in animation may need waiting; Compose test's performClick on a node after AnimatedVisibility... The test framework waits for idle. Should be fine; maybe use `waitUntil`.\n\nAlso the androidTest will use MainActivity which needs the app's Room DB — BudgetAppSmokeTest already does this, so fine.\n\nNow the JVM guard test. Name: `app/src/test/java/com/lorenzo/monefyme/ui/screen/HomeScreenSidebarWiringTest.kt`? Hmm, maybe better placed in a `ui/wiring` package? Keep it in `com.lorenzo.monefyme.ui.screen`.\n\nActually — think harder about a genuinely behavioural JVM test. What about testing CategoriesSidebar menu structure? The MenuBody composable has two entries hardcoded. I could extract the menu entries as data:\n\n```kotlin\ninternal data class SidebarMenuItem(val label: String, val action: () -> Unit)\n```\nand build `sidebarMenuItems(onOpenCategories, onOpenRestore)` as a pure function that MenuBody renders from a list. Then a JVM unit test can call that function and assert the Restore entry invokes onOpenRestore. That's a real behavioural test of the sidebar menu wiring and reasonably clean, not over-abstraction — modest refactor of MenuBody to iterate over entries. But it doesn't test that HomeScreen wires onOpenRestore.\n\nCombine: keep it simpler — source-based guard tests plus the real UI test in androidTest. I think I'll do both:\n1. JVM guard test: source-level wiring assertions (documented rationale), runnable by harness verify.\n2. androidTest UI test: real behaviour.\n\nLet me also double-check the \"Restore\" row in MenuBody — clicking the row calls onOpenRestore. Fine.\n\nAdditionally, should the sidebar's Restore item still show the Restore icon? Yes, task only says Home's balance-side buttons shouldn't have the Restore icon.\n\nNow write the code.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Now let me make the changes. First, `HomeScreen.kt`:"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_f80f36021ad54a9ca21bf712","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"C:\\p\\monefyme\\app\\src\\main\\java\\com\\lorenzo\\monefyme\\ui\\screen\\HomeScreen.kt","edits":[{"oldText":"import androidx.compose.material.icons.outlined.ChevronRight\nimport androidx.compose.material.icons.outlined.MoreVert\nimport androidx.compose.material.icons.outlined.Restore\n","newText":"import androidx.compose.material.icons.outlined.ChevronRight\nimport androidx.compose.material.icons.outlined.Menu\nimport androidx.compose.material.icons.outlined.MoreVert\n"},{"oldText":"            horizontalArrangement = Arrangement.SpaceBetween,\n        ) {\n            IconButton(\n                onClick = onOpenRestore,\n                modifier = Modifier.size(40.dp),\n            ) {\n                Icon(\n                    imageVector = Icons.Outlined.Restore,\n                    contentDescription = \"Open restore\",\n                    tint = Color(0xFF7CC8A4),\n                )\n            }\n            Surface(","newText":"            horizontalArrangement = Arrangement.SpaceBetween,\n        ) {\n            IconButton(\n                onClick = onOpenTransactionHistory,\n                modifier = Modifier\n                    .size(40.dp)\n                    .