 the merged node matches. In BudgetAppSmokeTest they used useUnmergedTree for tags on Surface. To be safe, use `useUnmergedTree = false` default with `onNodeWithTag(...).assert(hasContentDescription(...))` — risky. Instead: `onAllNodesWithContentDescription(\"Open transaction history\")` default tree.\n\nLet me write the androidTest carefully:\n\n```kotlin\npackage com.lorenzo.monefyme.ui\n\nimport androidx.compose.ui.test.assertIsDisplayed\nimport androidx.compose.ui.test.assertIsEnabled\nimport androidx.compose.ui.test.assert\nimport androidx.compose.ui.test.hasClickAction\nimport androidx.compose.ui.test.junit4.createAndroidComposeRule\nimport androidx.compose.ui.test.onNodeWithContentDescription\nimport androidx.compose.ui.test.onNodeWithTag\nimport androidx.compose.ui.test.onNodeWithText\nimport androidx.compose.ui.test.performClick\nimport com.lorenzo.monefyme.MainActivity\nimport org.junit.Rule\nimport org.junit.Test\n\nclass HomeRestoreNavigationTest {\n    @get:Rule\n    val composeTestRule = createAndroidComposeRule<MainActivity>()\n\n    @Test\n    fun sidebarRestoreItemOpensRestoreScreen() {\n        composeTestRule.onNodeWithContentDescription(\"More\").performClick()\n        composeTestRule.onNodeWithTag(\"sidebar_restore\", useUnmergedTree = true)\n            .assertIsDisplayed()\n            .performClick()\n        composeTestRule.onNodeWithText(\"Manual CSV restore\").assertIsDisplayed()\n    }\n```\nHmm the Row with testTag and clickable: the Row is clickable and has testTag; merged tree: Row is clickable, and Text children are merged... The testTag node exists in both trees. Use default tree (no useUnmergedTree). Simpler: `composeTestRule.onNodeWithText(\"Restore\").performClick()`. Text \"Restore\" only in sidebar. But wait — after navigating, Restore title \"Restore\" too. Just click text.\n\nWait careful: onNodeWithText(\"Restore\") before clicking - only sidebar row contains. OK, but is the sidebar text merged into the clickable Row? Text nodes are hidden in merged tree when merged into a clickable ancestor. Then onNodeWithText might not find it in merged tree! That's a known gotcha: text merged into a clickable parent gets hidden (text is merged but the child text node is hidden; onNodeWithText in merged tree matches the merged parent's text? The parent's config text would include \"Restore\" via mergeConfig of children — actually for clickable containers, SemanticsPropertyReceiver merges: the parent semantics node's text becomes combined children text? In Compose testing, when children are merged into parent, the parent's text is the concatenation of children texts. So onNodeWithText(\"Restore\") matches the parent row. Yes, merged text aggregation happens (that's why onNodeWithText works for buttons with Text children).\n\nSafest: use testTag with `performClick()` and default tree; testTag survives merging. And assert existence/ clickability of the tag.\n\nThen to verify navigation: assert \"Manual CSV restore\" displayed.\n\nSecond test: balance side buttons.\n\n```kotlin\n    @Test\n    fun balanceSideButtonsOpenTransactionHistory() {\n        composeTestRule.onNodeWithTag(\"balance_history_left\").assert(hasClickAction()).performClick()\n        composeTestRule.onNodeWithText(\"Transactions\").assertIsDisplayed()\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    fun balanceSideButtonsUseHamburgerIconsAndAreNotRestoreButtons() {\n        composeTestRule.onNodeWithContentDescription(\"Open transaction history\").assertCountEquals(2)\n        composeTestRule.onNodeWithContentDescription(\"Open restore\").assertDoesNotExist()\n    }\n```\n\nHmm: onNodeWithContentDescription(\"Open transaction history\") in merged tree — the IconButton parent merges the Icon's contentDescription. In merged tree the Icon child is hidden and its contentDescription merged into parent → matches parent, count 2. Good. But `onNodeWithContentDescription` throws if multiple? No — `onNodeWith...` throws if multiple match (\"Failed to find a single node\"). Use `onAllNodesWithContentDescription(...).assertCountEquals(2)`.\n\nAlso careful: does the sidebar (hidden, AnimatedVisibility not visible) exist? AnimatedVisibility offscreen removes it. Good.\n\nThird: assert restore icon buttons gone on Home: `onNodeWithContentDescription(\"Open restore\").assertDoesNotExist()`. On Home only; true after my change.\n\nNow, can I run these? Only with an emulator. Let me consider booting the emulator headless: `emulator -avd medium_phone -no-window -no-audio -gpu swiftshader_indirect` and then `gradlew connectedDebugAndroidTest`. This might work but could take a long time and the app's instrumentation needs a build. Time budget: session timeout 1500s (25 min). Worth trying — the harness rule says run tests yourself; but AGENTS.md explicitly says connectedDebugAndroidTest needs an emulator and is kept out of harness verify; \"run it manually in an unlocked session or when a task explicitly asks for it\". So I'm not required to run androidTest. I'll attempt the unit tests (required) and, if feasible, quickly try the emulator for the UI test compile-only check at minimum: `./gradlew connectedDebugAndroidTest` isn't possible; but `./gradlew assembleDebugAndroidTest` compiles the instrumentation tests — that verifies my androidTest compiles. That's a good middle ground.\n\n2. JVM unit test: a wiring guard test. Where to place: `app/src/test/java/com/lorenzo/monefyme/ui/screen/HomeScreenSidebarWiringTest.kt` — hmm name maybe `RestoreWiringTest`. Let's model it after GradleDeprecationWarningsTest with a `findProjectRoot()` helper.\n\nWhat should it assert?\n- HomeScreen.kt passes `onOpenRestore =` to the `CategoriesSidebar(` call (so sidebar Restore navigates).\n- CategoriesSidebar's `onOpenRestore` parameter has no default no-op (`= {}`), so callers must wire it (compile-time guarantee).\n- HomeScreen no longer references `Icons.Outlined.Restore` and its balance-side buttons use `Icons.Outlined.Menu` + `onClick = onOpenTransactionHistory`, with contentDescription \"Open transaction history\" appearing exactly twice.\n- Home screen still contains the sidebar Restore wiring.\n\nLet me write robustly: read file text, strip comments? Keep it simple.\n\nLet me write the test file now.\n\nAdditionally: should I also update `.harness-loop/tasks/01-bugfix-restore.md` status fields? The task file has `status: todo`; rules say do not edit files under .harness-loop other than the task file's own status fields. The runner probably updates status. I could set status to `done` at the end... Hmm, risky—the runner may manage attempts/status. Instructions mention \"the task file's own status fields\", implying it's allowed to update status. I'll leave the runner to do it; but maybe safer to not touch. Actually the archived-tasks pattern suggests runner moves them. I'll not modify.\n\nLet me write the unit test.\n"}}
